1
00:00:18,330 --> 00:00:21,330
My talk is "Flapping Birds and Space Telescopes."

2
00:00:21,330 --> 00:00:23,330
And you would think that should have nothing to do with one another,

3
00:00:23,330 --> 00:00:26,330
but I hope by the end of these 18 minutes,

4
00:00:26,330 --> 00:00:28,330
you'll see a little bit of a relation.

5
00:00:29,330 --> 00:00:30,330
It ties to origami. So let me start.

6
00:00:30,330 --> 00:00:32,330
What is origami?

7
00:00:32,330 --> 00:00:35,330
Most people think they know what origami is. It's this:

8
00:00:35,330 --> 00:00:38,330
flapping birds, toys, cootie catchers, that sort of thing.

9
00:00:38,330 --> 00:00:40,330
And that is what origami used to be.

10
00:00:40,330 --> 00:00:42,330
But it's become something else.

11
00:00:42,330 --> 00:00:44,330
It's become an art form, a form of sculpture.

12
00:00:44,330 --> 00:00:46,330
The common theme -- what makes it origami --

13
00:00:46,330 --> 00:00:50,330
is folding is how we create the form.

14
00:00:50,330 --> 00:00:53,330
You know, it's very old. This is a plate from 1797.

15
00:00:53,330 --> 00:00:55,330
It shows these women playing with these toys.

16
00:00:55,330 --> 00:00:58,330
If you look close, it's this shape, called a crane.

17
00:00:58,330 --> 00:01:00,330
Every Japanese kid

18
00:01:00,330 --> 00:01:02,330
learns how to fold that crane.

19
00:01:02,330 --> 00:01:04,330
So this art has been around for hundreds of years,

20
00:01:04,330 --> 00:01:06,330
and you would think something

21
00:01:06,330 --> 00:01:09,330
that's been around that long -- so restrictive, folding only --

22
00:01:09,330 --> 00:01:12,330
everything that could be done has been done a long time ago.

23
00:01:12,330 --> 00:01:14,330
And that might have been the case.

24
00:01:14,330 --> 00:01:16,330
But in the twentieth century,

25
00:01:16,330 --> 00:01:19,330
a Japanese folder named Yoshizawa came along,

26
00:01:19,330 --> 00:01:22,330
and he created tens of thousands of new designs.

27
00:01:22,330 --> 00:01:25,330
But even more importantly, he created a language,

28
00:01:25,330 --> 00:01:27,330
a way we could communicate,

29
00:01:27,330 --> 00:01:29,330
a code of dots, dashes and arrows.

30
00:01:29,330 --> 00:01:31,330
Harkening back to Susan Blackmore's talk,

31
00:01:31,330 --> 00:01:33,330
we now have a means of transmitting information

32
00:01:33,330 --> 00:01:36,330
with heredity and selection,

33
00:01:36,330 --> 00:01:38,330
and we know where that leads.

34
00:01:38,330 --> 00:01:40,330
And where it has led in origami

35
00:01:40,330 --> 00:01:42,330
is to things like this.

36
00:01:42,330 --> 00:01:44,330
This is an origami figure --

37
00:01:44,330 --> 00:01:48,330
one sheet, no cuts, folding only, hundreds of folds.

38
00:01:50,330 --> 00:01:52,330
This, too, is origami,

39
00:01:52,330 --> 00:01:55,330
and this shows where we've gone in the modern world.

40
00:01:55,330 --> 00:01:57,330
Naturalism. Detail.

41
00:01:57,330 --> 00:01:59,330
You can get horns, antlers --

42
00:01:59,330 --> 00:02:01,330
even, if you look close, cloven hooves.

43
00:02:01,330 --> 00:02:04,330
And it raises a question: what changed?

44
00:02:04,330 --> 00:02:06,330
And what changed is something

45
00:02:06,330 --> 00:02:09,330
you might not have expected in an art,

46
00:02:09,330 --> 00:02:11,330
which is math.

47
00:02:11,330 --> 00:02:13,330
That is, people applied mathematical principles

48
00:02:13,330 --> 00:02:16,330
to the art,

49
00:02:16,330 --> 00:02:18,330
to discover the underlying laws.

50
00:02:18,330 --> 00:02:21,330
And that leads to a very powerful tool.

51
00:02:21,330 --> 00:02:23,330
The secret to productivity in so many fields --

52
00:02:23,330 --> 00:02:25,330
and in origami --

53
00:02:25,330 --> 00:02:28,330
is letting dead people do your work for you.

54
00:02:28,330 --> 00:02:29,330
(Laughter)

55
00:02:29,330 --> 00:02:31,330
Because what you can do is

56
00:02:31,330 --> 00:02:33,330
take your problem,

57
00:02:33,330 --> 00:02:36,330
and turn it into a problem that someone else has solved,

58
00:02:36,330 --> 00:02:38,330
and use their solutions.

59
00:02:38,330 --> 00:02:41,330
And I want to tell you how we did that in origami.

60
00:02:41,330 --> 00:02:43,330
Origami revolves around crease patterns.

61
00:02:43,330 --> 00:02:45,330
The crease pattern shown here is the underlying blueprint

62
00:02:46,330 --> 00:02:48,330
for an origami figure.

63
00:02:48,330 --> 00:02:50,330
And you can't just draw them arbitrarily.

64
00:02:50,330 --> 00:02:53,330
They have to obey four simple laws.

65
00:02:53,330 --> 00:02:55,330
And they're very simple, easy to understand.

66
00:02:55,330 --> 00:02:58,330
The first law is two-colorability. You can color any crease pattern

67
00:02:58,330 --> 00:03:00,330
with just two colors without ever having

68
00:03:00,330 --> 00:03:03,330
the same color meeting.

69
00:03:03,330 --> 00:03:06,330
The directions of the folds at any vertex --

70
00:03:06,330 --> 00:03:09,330
the number of mountain folds, the number of valley folds --

71
00:03:09,330 --> 00:03:11,330
always differs by two. Two more or two less.

72
00:03:11,330 --> 00:03:13,330
Nothing else.

73
00:03:13,330 --> 00:03:15,330
If you look at the angles around the fold,

74
00:03:15,330 --> 00:03:17,330
you find that if you number the angles in a circle,

75
00:03:17,330 --> 00:03:20,330
all the even-numbered angles add up to a straight line,

76
00:03:20,330 --> 00:03:23,330
all the odd-numbered angles add up to a straight line.

77
00:03:23,330 --> 00:03:25,330
And if you look at how the layers stack,

78
00:03:25,330 --> 00:03:28,330
you'll find that no matter how you stack folds and sheets,

79
00:03:28,330 --> 00:03:30,330
a sheet can never

80
00:03:30,330 --> 00:03:32,330
penetrate a fold.

81
00:03:32,330 --> 00:03:35,330
So that's four simple laws. That's all you need in origami.

82
00:03:35,330 --> 00:03:37,330
All of origami comes from that.

83
00:03:37,330 --> 00:03:39,330
And you'd think, "Can four simple laws

84
00:03:39,330 --> 00:03:41,330
give rise to that kind of complexity?"

85
00:03:41,330 --> 00:03:43,330
But indeed, the laws of quantum mechanics

86
00:03:43,330 --> 00:03:45,330
can be written down on a napkin,

87
00:03:45,330 --> 00:03:47,330
and yet they govern all of chemistry,

88
00:03:47,330 --> 00:03:49,330
all of life, all of history.

89
00:03:49,330 --> 00:03:51,330
If we obey these laws,

90
00:03:51,330 --> 00:03:53,330
we can do amazing things.

91
00:03:53,330 --> 00:03:55,330
So in origami, to obey these laws,

92
00:03:55,330 --> 00:03:57,330
we can take simple patterns --

93
00:03:57,330 --> 00:04:00,330
like this repeating pattern of folds, called textures --

94
00:04:00,330 --> 00:04:02,330
and by itself it's nothing.

95
00:04:02,330 --> 00:04:04,330
But if we follow the laws of origami,

96
00:04:04,330 --> 00:04:07,330
we can put these patterns into another fold

97
00:04:07,330 --> 00:04:09,330
that itself might be something very, very simple,

98
00:04:09,330 --> 00:04:11,330
but when we put it together,

99
00:04:11,330 --> 00:04:13,330
we get something a little different.

100
00:04:13,330 --> 00:04:16,330
This fish, 400 scales --

101
00:04:16,330 --> 00:04:19,330
again, it is one uncut square, only folding.

102
00:04:20,330 --> 00:04:22,330
And if you don't want to fold 400 scales,

103
00:04:22,330 --> 00:04:24,330
you can back off and just do a few things,

104
00:04:24,330 --> 00:04:27,330
and add plates to the back of a turtle, or toes.

105
00:04:27,330 --> 00:04:30,330
Or you can ramp up and go up to 50 stars

106
00:04:30,330 --> 00:04:33,330
on a flag, with 13 stripes.

107
00:04:33,330 --> 00:04:36,330
And if you want to go really crazy,

108
00:04:36,330 --> 00:04:38,330
1,000 scales on a rattlesnake.

109
00:04:38,330 --> 00:04:40,330
And this guy's on display downstairs,

110
00:04:40,330 --> 00:04:43,330
so take a look if you get a chance.

111
00:04:43,330 --> 00:04:45,330
The most powerful tools in origami

112
00:04:45,330 --> 00:04:48,330
have related to how we get parts of creatures.

113
00:04:48,330 --> 00:04:50,330
And I can put it in this simple equation.

114
00:04:50,330 --> 00:04:52,330
We take an idea,

115
00:04:52,330 --> 00:04:55,330
combine it with a square, and you get an origami figure.

116
00:04:55,330 --> 00:04:59,330
(Laughter)

117
00:04:59,330 --> 00:05:01,330
What matters is what we mean by those symbols.

118
00:05:01,330 --> 00:05:04,330
And you might say, "Can you really be that specific?

119
00:05:04,330 --> 00:05:06,330
I mean, a stag beetle -- it's got two points for jaws,

120
00:05:06,330 --> 00:05:10,330
it's got antennae. Can you be that specific in the detail?"

121
00:05:10,330 --> 00:05:13,330
And yeah, you really can.

122
00:05:13,330 --> 00:05:16,330
So how do we do that? Well, we break it down

123
00:05:16,330 --> 00:05:18,330
into a few smaller steps.

124
00:05:18,330 --> 00:05:20,330
So let me stretch out that equation.

125
00:05:20,330 --> 00:05:23,330
I start with my idea. I abstract it.

126
00:05:23,330 --> 00:05:26,330
What's the most abstract form? It's a stick figure.

127
00:05:26,330 --> 00:05:29,330
And from that stick figure, I somehow have to get to a folded shape

128
00:05:29,330 --> 00:05:32,330
that has a part for every bit of the subject,

129
00:05:32,330 --> 00:05:34,330
a flap for every leg.

130
00:05:34,330 --> 00:05:37,330
And then once I have that folded shape that we call the base,

131
00:05:37,330 --> 00:05:40,330
you can make the legs narrower, you can bend them,

132
00:05:40,330 --> 00:05:42,330
you can turn it into the finished shape.

133
00:05:42,330 --> 00:05:44,330
Now the first step, pretty easy.

134
00:05:44,330 --> 00:05:46,330
Take an idea, draw a stick figure.

135
00:05:46,330 --> 00:05:49,330
The last step is not so hard, but that middle step --

136
00:05:49,330 --> 00:05:52,330
going from the abstract description to the folded shape --

137
00:05:52,330 --> 00:05:54,330
that's hard.

138
00:05:54,330 --> 00:05:56,330
But that's the place where the mathematical ideas

139
00:05:56,330 --> 00:05:58,330
can get us over the hump.

140
00:05:58,330 --> 00:06:00,330
And I'm going to show you all how to do that

141
00:06:00,330 --> 00:06:02,330
so you can go out of here and fold something.

142
00:06:02,330 --> 00:06:04,330
But we're going to start small.

143
00:06:04,330 --> 00:06:06,330
This base has a lot of flaps in it.

144
00:06:06,330 --> 00:06:09,330
We're going to learn how to make one flap.

145
00:06:09,330 --> 00:06:11,330
How would you make a single flap?

146
00:06:11,330 --> 00:06:14,330
Take a square. Fold it in half, fold it in half, fold it again,

147
00:06:14,330 --> 00:06:16,330
until it gets long and narrow,

148
00:06:16,330 --> 00:06:18,330
and then we'll say at the end of that, that's a flap.

149
00:06:18,330 --> 00:06:21,330
I could use that for a leg, an arm, anything like that.

150
00:06:21,330 --> 00:06:23,330
What paper went into that flap?

151
00:06:23,330 --> 00:06:25,330
Well, if I unfold it and go back to the crease pattern,

152
00:06:25,330 --> 00:06:28,330
you can see that the upper left corner of that shape

153
00:06:28,330 --> 00:06:30,330
is the paper that went into the flap.

154
00:06:30,330 --> 00:06:33,330
So that's the flap, and all the rest of the paper's left over.

155
00:06:33,330 --> 00:06:35,330
I can use it for something else.

156
00:06:35,330 --> 00:06:37,330
Well, there are other ways of making a flap.

157
00:06:37,330 --> 00:06:39,330
There are other dimensions for flaps.

158
00:06:39,330 --> 00:06:42,330
If I make the flaps skinnier, I can use a bit less paper.

159
00:06:42,330 --> 00:06:45,330
If I make the flap as skinny as possible,

160
00:06:45,330 --> 00:06:48,330
I get to the limit of the minimum amount of paper needed.

161
00:06:48,330 --> 00:06:51,330
And you can see there, it needs a quarter-circle of paper to make a flap.

162
00:06:52,330 --> 00:06:54,330
There's other ways of making flaps.

163
00:06:54,330 --> 00:06:57,330
If I put the flap on the edge, it uses a half circle of paper.

164
00:06:57,330 --> 00:07:00,330
And if I make the flap from the middle, it uses a full circle.

165
00:07:00,330 --> 00:07:02,330
So, no matter how I make a flap,

166
00:07:02,330 --> 00:07:04,330
it needs some part

167
00:07:04,330 --> 00:07:06,330
of a circular region of paper.

168
00:07:06,330 --> 00:07:08,330
So now we're ready to scale up.

169
00:07:08,330 --> 00:07:11,330
What if I want to make something that has a lot of flaps?

170
00:07:11,330 --> 00:07:14,330
What do I need? I need a lot of circles.

171
00:07:15,330 --> 00:07:17,330
And in the 1990s,

172
00:07:17,330 --> 00:07:19,330
origami artists discovered these principles

173
00:07:19,330 --> 00:07:22,330
and realized we could make arbitrarily complicated figures

174
00:07:22,330 --> 00:07:25,330
just by packing circles.

175
00:07:25,330 --> 00:07:28,330
And here's where the dead people start to help us out,

176
00:07:28,330 --> 00:07:31,330
because lots of people have studied

177
00:07:31,330 --> 00:07:33,330
the problem of packing circles.

178
00:07:33,330 --> 00:07:36,330
I can rely on that vast history of mathematicians and artists

179
00:07:36,330 --> 00:07:39,330
looking at disc packings and arrangements.

180
00:07:39,330 --> 00:07:42,330
And I can use those patterns now to create origami shapes.

181
00:07:43,330 --> 00:07:45,330
So we figured out these rules whereby you pack circles,

182
00:07:45,330 --> 00:07:48,330
you decorate the patterns of circles with lines

183
00:07:48,330 --> 00:07:50,330
according to more rules. That gives you the folds.

184
00:07:50,330 --> 00:07:53,330
Those folds fold into a base. You shape the base.

185
00:07:53,330 --> 00:07:56,330
You get a folded shape -- in this case, a cockroach.

186
00:07:57,330 --> 00:07:59,330
And it's so simple.

187
00:07:59,330 --> 00:08:02,330
(Laughter)

188
00:08:02,330 --> 00:08:05,330
It's so simple that a computer could do it.

189
00:08:05,330 --> 00:08:07,330
And you say, "Well, you know, how simple is that?"

190
00:08:07,330 --> 00:08:09,330
But computers -- you need to be able to describe things

191
00:08:09,330 --> 00:08:12,330
in very basic terms, and with this, we could.

192
00:08:12,330 --> 00:08:14,330
So I wrote a computer program a bunch of years ago

193
00:08:14,330 --> 00:08:16,330
called TreeMaker, and you can download it from my website.

194
00:08:16,330 --> 00:08:19,330
It's free. It runs on all the major platforms -- even Windows.

195
00:08:19,330 --> 00:08:21,330
(Laughter)

196
00:08:21,330 --> 00:08:23,330
And you just draw a stick figure,

197
00:08:23,330 --> 00:08:25,330
and it calculates the crease pattern.

198
00:08:25,330 --> 00:08:28,330
It does the circle packing, calculates the crease pattern,

199
00:08:28,330 --> 00:08:30,330
and if you use that stick figure that I just showed --

200
00:08:30,330 --> 00:08:33,330
which you can kind of tell, it's a deer, it's got antlers --

201
00:08:33,330 --> 00:08:35,330
you'll get this crease pattern.

202
00:08:35,330 --> 00:08:37,330
And if you take this crease pattern, you fold on the dotted lines,

203
00:08:37,330 --> 00:08:40,330
you'll get a base that you can then shape

204
00:08:40,330 --> 00:08:42,330
into a deer,

205
00:08:42,330 --> 00:08:44,330
with exactly the crease pattern that you wanted.

206
00:08:44,330 --> 00:08:46,330
And if you want a different deer,

207
00:08:46,330 --> 00:08:49,330
not a white-tailed deer, but you want a mule deer, or an elk,

208
00:08:49,330 --> 00:08:51,330
you change the packing,

209
00:08:51,330 --> 00:08:53,330
and you can do an elk.

210
00:08:53,330 --> 00:08:55,330
Or you could do a moose.

211
00:08:55,330 --> 00:08:57,330
Or, really, any other kind of deer.

212
00:08:57,330 --> 00:09:00,330
These techniques revolutionized this art.

213
00:09:00,330 --> 00:09:02,330
We found we could do insects,

214
00:09:02,330 --> 00:09:04,330
spiders, which are close,

215
00:09:04,330 --> 00:09:07,330
things with legs, things with legs and wings,

216
00:09:08,330 --> 00:09:10,330
things with legs and antennae.

217
00:09:10,330 --> 00:09:13,330
And if folding a single praying mantis from a single uncut square

218
00:09:13,330 --> 00:09:15,330
wasn't interesting enough,

219
00:09:15,330 --> 00:09:17,330
then you could do two praying mantises

220
00:09:17,330 --> 00:09:19,330
from a single uncut square.

221
00:09:19,330 --> 00:09:21,330
She's eating him.

222
00:09:21,330 --> 00:09:24,330
I call it "Snack Time."

223
00:09:24,330 --> 00:09:26,330
And you can do more than just insects.

224
00:09:26,330 --> 00:09:28,330
This -- you can put details,

225
00:09:28,330 --> 00:09:31,330
toes and claws. A grizzly bear has claws.

226
00:09:31,330 --> 00:09:33,330
This tree frog has toes.

227
00:09:33,330 --> 00:09:36,330
Actually, lots of people in origami now put toes into their models.

228
00:09:36,330 --> 00:09:38,330
Toes have become an origami meme,

229
00:09:38,330 --> 00:09:41,330
because everyone's doing it.

230
00:09:41,330 --> 00:09:43,330
You can make multiple subjects.

231
00:09:43,330 --> 00:09:45,330
So these are a couple of instrumentalists.

232
00:09:45,330 --> 00:09:48,330
The guitar player from a single square,

233
00:09:48,330 --> 00:09:50,330
the bass player from a single square.

234
00:09:50,330 --> 00:09:52,330
And if you say, "Well, but the guitar, bass --

235
00:09:52,330 --> 00:09:54,330
that's not so hot.

236
00:09:54,330 --> 00:09:56,330
Do a little more complicated instrument."

237
00:09:56,330 --> 00:09:58,330
Well, then you could do an organ.

238
00:09:58,330 --> 00:10:01,330
(Laughter)

239
00:10:01,330 --> 00:10:03,330
And what this has allowed is the creation

240
00:10:03,330 --> 00:10:05,330
of origami-on-demand.

241
00:10:05,330 --> 00:10:08,330
So now people can say, "I want exactly this and this and this,"

242
00:10:08,330 --> 00:10:11,330
and you can go out and fold it.

243
00:10:11,330 --> 00:10:13,330
And sometimes you create high art,

244
00:10:13,330 --> 00:10:16,330
and sometimes you pay the bills by doing some commercial work.

245
00:10:16,330 --> 00:10:18,330
But I want to show you some examples.

246
00:10:18,330 --> 00:10:20,330
Everything you'll see here,

247
00:10:20,330 --> 00:10:23,330
except the car, is origami.

248
00:10:23,330 --> 00:10:51,330
(Video)

249
00:10:51,330 --> 00:10:54,330
(Applause)

250
00:10:54,330 --> 00:10:57,330
Just to show you, this really was folded paper.

251
00:10:57,330 --> 00:10:59,330
Computers made things move,

252
00:10:59,330 --> 00:11:02,330
but these were all real, folded objects that we made.

253
00:11:03,330 --> 00:11:06,330
And we can use this not just for visuals,

254
00:11:06,330 --> 00:11:09,330
but it turns out to be useful even in the real world.

255
00:11:09,330 --> 00:11:10,330
Surprisingly, origami

256
00:11:10,330 --> 00:11:13,330
and the structures that we've developed in origami

257
00:11:13,330 --> 00:11:16,330
turn out to have applications in medicine, in science,

258
00:11:16,330 --> 00:11:19,330
in space, in the body, consumer electronics and more.

259
00:11:19,330 --> 00:11:22,330
And I want to show you some of these examples.

260
00:11:22,330 --> 00:11:24,330
One of the earliest was this pattern,

261
00:11:24,330 --> 00:11:26,330
this folded pattern,

262
00:11:26,330 --> 00:11:29,330
studied by Koryo Miura, a Japanese engineer.

263
00:11:29,330 --> 00:11:31,330
He studied a folding pattern, and realized

264
00:11:31,330 --> 00:11:34,330
this could fold down into an extremely compact package

265
00:11:34,330 --> 00:11:37,330
that had a very simple opening and closing structure.

266
00:11:37,330 --> 00:11:40,330
And he used it to design this solar array.

267
00:11:40,330 --> 00:11:43,330
It's an artist's rendition, but it flew in a Japanese telescope

268
00:11:43,330 --> 00:11:45,330
in 1995.

269
00:11:45,330 --> 00:11:47,330
Now, there is actually a little origami

270
00:11:47,330 --> 00:11:50,330
in the James Webb Space Telescope, but it's very simple.

271
00:11:50,330 --> 00:11:52,330
The telescope, going up in space,

272
00:11:52,330 --> 00:11:55,330
it unfolds in two places.

273
00:11:55,330 --> 00:11:57,330
It folds in thirds. It's a very simple pattern --

274
00:11:57,330 --> 00:11:59,330
you wouldn't even call that origami.

275
00:11:59,330 --> 00:12:02,330
They certainly didn't need to talk to origami artists.

276
00:12:02,330 --> 00:12:05,330
But if you want to go higher and go larger than this,

277
00:12:05,330 --> 00:12:07,330
then you might need some origami.

278
00:12:07,330 --> 00:12:09,330
Engineers at Lawrence Livermore National Lab

279
00:12:09,330 --> 00:12:12,330
had an idea for a telescope much larger.

280
00:12:12,330 --> 00:12:14,330
They called it the Eyeglass.

281
00:12:14,330 --> 00:12:16,330
The design called for geosynchronous orbit

282
00:12:16,330 --> 00:12:18,330
25,000 miles up,

283
00:12:18,330 --> 00:12:21,330
100-meter diameter lens.

284
00:12:21,330 --> 00:12:24,330
So, imagine a lens the size of a football field.

285
00:12:24,330 --> 00:12:26,330
There were two groups of people who were interested in this:

286
00:12:26,330 --> 00:12:29,330
planetary scientists, who want to look up,

287
00:12:29,330 --> 00:12:32,330
and then other people, who wanted to look down.

288
00:12:33,330 --> 00:12:35,330
Whether you look up or look down,

289
00:12:35,330 --> 00:12:38,330
how do you get it up in space? You've got to get it up there in a rocket.

290
00:12:38,330 --> 00:12:41,330
And rockets are small. So you have to make it smaller.

291
00:12:41,330 --> 00:12:43,330
How do you make a large sheet of glass smaller?

292
00:12:43,330 --> 00:12:46,330
Well, about the only way is to fold it up somehow.

293
00:12:46,330 --> 00:12:48,330
So you have to do something like this.

294
00:12:48,330 --> 00:12:50,330
This was a small model.

295
00:12:51,330 --> 00:12:53,330
Folded lens, you divide up the panels, you add flexures.

296
00:12:53,330 --> 00:12:56,330
But this pattern's not going to work

297
00:12:56,330 --> 00:12:59,330
to get something 100 meters down to a few meters.

298
00:12:59,330 --> 00:13:01,330
So the Livermore engineers,

299
00:13:01,330 --> 00:13:03,330
wanting to make use of the work of dead people,

300
00:13:03,330 --> 00:13:06,330
or perhaps live origamists, said,

301
00:13:06,330 --> 00:13:09,330
"Let's see if someone else is doing this sort of thing."

302
00:13:09,330 --> 00:13:12,330
So they looked into the origami community,

303
00:13:12,330 --> 00:13:14,330
we got in touch with them, and I started working with them.

304
00:13:14,330 --> 00:13:16,330
And we developed a pattern together

305
00:13:16,330 --> 00:13:18,330
that scales to arbitrarily large size,

306
00:13:18,330 --> 00:13:22,330
but that allows any flat ring or disc

307
00:13:22,330 --> 00:13:25,330
to fold down into a very neat, compact cylinder.

308
00:13:25,330 --> 00:13:27,330
And they adopted that for their first generation,

309
00:13:27,330 --> 00:13:29,330
which was not 100 meters -- it was a five-meter.

310
00:13:29,330 --> 00:13:31,330
But this is a five-meter telescope --

311
00:13:31,330 --> 00:13:33,330
has about a quarter-mile focal length.

312
00:13:33,330 --> 00:13:35,330
And it works perfectly on its test range,

313
00:13:35,330 --> 00:13:38,330
and it indeed folds up into a neat little bundle.

314
00:13:39,330 --> 00:13:41,330
Now, there is other origami in space.

315
00:13:41,330 --> 00:13:44,330
Japan Aerospace [Exploration] Agency flew a solar sail,

316
00:13:44,330 --> 00:13:47,330
and you can see here that the sail expands out,

317
00:13:47,330 --> 00:13:49,330
and you can still see the fold lines.

318
00:13:49,330 --> 00:13:52,330
The problem that's being solved here is

319
00:13:52,330 --> 00:13:55,330
something that needs to be big and sheet-like at its destination,

320
00:13:55,330 --> 00:13:57,330
but needs to be small for the journey.

321
00:13:57,330 --> 00:14:00,330
And that works whether you're going into space,

322
00:14:00,330 --> 00:14:03,330
or whether you're just going into a body.

323
00:14:03,330 --> 00:14:05,330
And this example is the latter.

324
00:14:05,330 --> 00:14:08,330
This is a heart stent developed by Zhong You

325
00:14:08,330 --> 00:14:10,330
at Oxford University.

326
00:14:10,330 --> 00:14:13,330
It holds open a blocked artery when it gets to its destination,

327
00:14:13,330 --> 00:14:16,330
but it needs to be much smaller for the trip there,

328
00:14:16,330 --> 00:14:18,330
through your blood vessels.

329
00:14:18,330 --> 00:14:21,330
And this stent folds down using an origami pattern,

330
00:14:21,330 --> 00:14:24,330
based on a model called the water bomb base.

331
00:14:25,330 --> 00:14:27,330
Airbag designers also have the problem

332
00:14:27,330 --> 00:14:29,330
of getting flat sheets

333
00:14:29,330 --> 00:14:32,330
into a small space.

334
00:14:32,330 --> 00:14:34,330
And they want to do their design by simulation.

335
00:14:34,330 --> 00:14:36,330
So they need to figure out how, in a computer,

336
00:14:36,330 --> 00:14:38,330
to flatten an airbag.

337
00:14:38,330 --> 00:14:40,330
And the algorithms that we developed

338
00:14:40,330 --> 00:14:42,330
to do insects

339
00:14:42,330 --> 00:14:45,330
turned out to be the solution for airbags

340
00:14:45,330 --> 00:14:47,330
to do their simulation.

341
00:14:47,330 --> 00:14:50,330
And so they can do a simulation like this.

342
00:14:50,330 --> 00:14:52,330
Those are the origami creases forming,

343
00:14:52,330 --> 00:14:54,330
and now you can see the airbag inflate

344
00:14:54,330 --> 00:14:57,330
and find out, does it work?

345
00:14:57,330 --> 00:14:59,330
And that leads

346
00:14:59,330 --> 00:15:01,330
to a really interesting idea.

347
00:15:01,330 --> 00:15:04,330
You know, where did these things come from?

348
00:15:04,330 --> 00:15:06,330
Well, the heart stent

349
00:15:06,330 --> 00:15:08,330
came from that little blow-up box

350
00:15:08,330 --> 00:15:11,330
that you might have learned in elementary school.

351
00:15:11,330 --> 00:15:14,330
It's the same pattern, called the water bomb base.

352
00:15:14,330 --> 00:15:16,330
The airbag-flattening algorithm

353
00:15:16,330 --> 00:15:18,330
came from all the developments

354
00:15:18,330 --> 00:15:21,330
of circle packing and the mathematical theory

355
00:15:21,330 --> 00:15:23,330
that was really developed

356
00:15:23,330 --> 00:15:26,330
just to create insects -- things with legs.

357
00:15:27,330 --> 00:15:29,330
The thing is, that this often happens

358
00:15:29,330 --> 00:15:31,330
in math and science.

359
00:15:31,330 --> 00:15:34,330
When you get math involved, problems that you solve

360
00:15:34,330 --> 00:15:36,330
for aesthetic value only,

361
00:15:36,330 --> 00:15:38,330
or to create something beautiful,

362
00:15:38,330 --> 00:15:40,330
turn around and turn out

363
00:15:40,330 --> 00:15:43,330
to have an application in the real world.

364
00:15:43,330 --> 00:15:46,330
And as weird and surprising as it may sound,

365
00:15:46,330 --> 00:15:49,330
origami may someday even save a life.

366
00:15:50,330 --> 00:15:52,330
Thanks.

367
00:15:52,330 --> 00:15:54,330
(Applause)

